    "q-75826": {
      "dom_id": "step-q75826",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2x+1}{4{x}^{2}+5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+5x+1=(4x+1)(x+1).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+1}{4{x}^{2}+5x+1} & =\\frac{2x+1}{(4x+1)(x+1)}=\\frac{A}{4x+1}+\\frac{B}{x+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n2x+1=A(x+1)+B(4x+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-1)+1 & =A(-1+1)+B(4(-1)+1) \\\\ -1 & =-3B \\\\ B & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{4},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-\\frac{1}{4})+1 & =A(-\\frac{1}{4}+1)+B(4(-\\frac{1}{4})+1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{3}{4}A \\\\ A & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x+1}{4{x}^{2}+5x+1}=\\frac{2}{3(4x+1)}+\\frac{1}{3(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+1}{4{x}^{2}+5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{3(4x+1)}+\\frac{1}{3(x+1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{4}{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x+1|+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+1}{4{x}^{2}+5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x+1|+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{4x+1}+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+1)\\sqrt{4x+1}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2x+1}{4{x}^{2}+5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+1)\\sqrt{4x+1}|{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\sqrt{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
